<?xml version="1.0"?>
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

    <!--
        REBEL-6DOF-04 gripper (gripper_type:=v2).

        Built from the CAD export "full gripper with bucket finger.glb" plus the
        two single-side fingertips "maintanance_finger.glb" and
        "probe_finger.glb".  scripts/build_gripper_v2_meshes.py splits that
        assembly into the per-link meshes this file loads and prints every
        number quoted below, so all of it can be regenerated rather than
        trusted.

        WHAT CHANGED FROM gripper_new.xacro
        The base casting is the same part - its mesh lands inside 0.5 mm of
        gripper_base.stl in every Z slab, which is what pins the frame below -
        and every link and joint NAME is unchanged, so this file is a drop-in
        swap.  The linkage inside it is not the same:

          - Both jaws are now driven by MESHING SECTOR GEARS on a central
            servo pinion, so they counter-rotate as one degree of freedom
            rather than through five same-sign mimics.
          - Each jaw is an exact PARALLELOGRAM: 50.000 mm bars on a 35.000 mm
            ground link (measured 49.991-50.004 and 34.999).  gripper_new's
            bars were unequal, 85.563 against a 35.11 ground link, which is
            why that file has to explain at length why both bars still have to
            use the same 85.563 tip offset.  Here the parallelogram is real,
            so the fingertip TRANSLATES: its orientation is fixed for every q
            and the jaw faces stay parallel through the whole stroke.
          - Because the fingertip does not rotate, its joint mimics the driver
            at -1.0, cancelling the bar it hangs off.

        STROKE.  0 to 83 mm, against gripper_new's 0 to 183 mm.  The command
        convention is preserved - q = +0.07 rad closes, q < 0 opens - and the
        joint zero is calibrated so the bucket jaws touch exactly at +0.07
        (see ZERO-POSE below).  But the two grippers are NOT interchangeable
        at the same joint angle, and anything that converts an object width
        into a close angle must be re-fitted:

          jaw gap [mm] against q [rad]
            q       -1.373 -1.200 -1.000 -0.800 -0.600 -0.400 -0.200 -0.100  0.000  0.070
            bucket    83.1   81.7   76.3   67.4   55.2   40.4   24.0   15.4    6.3    0.0
            maint.    76.4   74.9   69.5   60.5   48.0   32.7   15.2    5.8    0.0      -
            probe     75.4   73.9   68.5   59.4   47.0   31.7   14.2    4.8    0.0      -

          jaw contact sits at z = 243 / 215 / 224 mm from arm_gripper_base_link
          for bucket / maintenance / probe.

        TWO CAVEATS worth knowing before trusting a commanded angle:

        1. The gap is NOT monotonic over the whole joint range.  The bars pass
           through perpendicular - over centre - at q = -1.3728, and past that
           the jaws start closing again (81.7 mm at -1.2, 83.1 mm at -1.373,
           and back to ~81 mm at the -1.57 limit).  The limit is kept at -1.57
           because the linkage really does reach it with nothing fouling, and
           because the rest of the stack calls -1.57 "open"; but -1.373 is the
           widest the gripper actually opens, and any gap-to-angle inverse must
           be restricted to q >= -1.373 or it can pick the wrong branch.
        2. The three fingertips do NOT close at the same angle.  gripper_new's
           three happened to agree within 0.5 mm; these do not - maintenance
           touches at q ~ -0.030 and probe at q ~ -0.045, both well before the
           bucket's +0.07.  The joint limit stays at +0.07 (it is a property of
           the linkage, not of the tip), so with those tips fitted a commanded
           "closed" drives them into contact ~7 mm early.
    -->

    <!-- Keep the nominal -1.57/0.07 commands inside Gazebo's physics limits.
         When a commanded endpoint equals a DART hard stop, the linked mimic
         joints can wedge there and refuse the following reverse command. Real
         hardware retains its original physical limits. -->
    <xacro:property name="gripper_joint_lower_limit"
                    value="${-1.62 if gripper_hardware_protocol == 'gazebo' else -1.57}"/>
    <xacro:property name="gripper_joint_upper_limit"
                    value="${0.12 if gripper_hardware_protocol == 'gazebo' else 0.07}"/>

    <!-- The fingertip joints mimic the driver with multiplier -1.0, so their
         limits must be transformed by the same relation.  Reusing the driver
         limits makes every open pose invalid in MoveIt: driver q=-1.57 maps
         to fingertip q=+1.57, which used to exceed the fingertip's +0.12
         Gazebo limit. -->
    <xacro:property name="gripper_bucket_joint_lower_limit"
                    value="${-gripper_joint_upper_limit}"/>
    <xacro:property name="gripper_bucket_joint_upper_limit"
                    value="${-gripper_joint_lower_limit}"/>

    <!-- ZERO POSE.  The bars sit 11.3428 deg off the gripper axis at q = 0,
         which is the one free choice in the whole model: the CAD snapshot is a
         pose, not a datum, and its two jaws are not even symmetric with each
         other (the bars are at -14.031 and +12.627 deg in the export).  The
         angle below is solved so the bucket jaws just touch at q = +0.07,
         which is what "closed" means everywhere else in this stack - the SRDF
         `closed` group state, the mock-hardware command limits in
         igus_rebel2.control.xacro, the aries_vision_grasp gap tables.  It sits
         2.0 deg from the symmetrised CAD pose, inside that pose's own 1.4 deg
         left-right disagreement.

         Everything downstream is a consequence: sin/cos of this angle times
         the 50 mm bar give the tip offsets, so change it here and re-run the
         build script rather than editing the offsets by hand. -->
    <xacro:property name="fourbar_zero_psi" value="${radians(11.3428)}"/>
    <xacro:property name="fourbar_bar" value="0.050"/>
    <xacro:property name="fourbar_tip_x" value="${fourbar_bar * sin(fourbar_zero_psi)}"/>
    <xacro:property name="fourbar_tip_z" value="${fourbar_bar * cos(fourbar_zero_psi)}"/>

    <!-- Physically swappable fingertip. finger_type is 'bucket', 'maintenance'
         or 'probe'. Unlike gripper_new.xacro these are handed: the two jaws are
         mirror images about the gripper axis, and a mirror is not a rotation,
         so a left and a right mesh are exported for each type. The bucket pair
         comes straight out of the assembly; the other two are the single-side
         export placed on both jaws, which is only valid because each of those
         solids is symmetric about its own mid-plane (the build script asserts
         it). -->
    <xacro:property name="finger_mesh_left" value="gripper_${finger_type}_left.stl"/>
    <xacro:property name="finger_mesh_right" value="gripper_${finger_type}_right.stl"/>

    <!-- Mass properties integrated from each exported mesh at 1050 kg/m^3
         (near-solid printed plastic), the same density gripper_new.xacro used
         so the two models stay comparable. COM and inertia are about that COM.
         The left-hand values are listed; the right-hand link is the mirror, so
         it takes -cx and -ixz and leaves the rest alone. Total gripper mass is
         458 g with the bucket fitted, of which 90 g is the servo. -->
    <xacro:property name="finger_mass"
                    value="${0.0277 if finger_type == 'probe' else (0.0433 if finger_type == 'bucket' else 0.0187)}"/>
    <xacro:property name="finger_com_x"
                    value="${0.01892 if finger_type == 'probe' else (0.00840 if finger_type == 'bucket' else 0.01748)}"/>
    <xacro:property name="finger_com_z"
                    value="${0.05805 if finger_type == 'probe' else (0.05452 if finger_type == 'bucket' else 0.04608)}"/>
    <xacro:property name="finger_ixx"
                    value="${2.343e-05 if finger_type == 'probe' else (3.617e-05 if finger_type == 'bucket' else 1.338e-05)}"/>
    <xacro:property name="finger_iyy"
                    value="${2.362e-05 if finger_type == 'probe' else (3.212e-05 if finger_type == 'bucket' else 1.468e-05)}"/>
    <xacro:property name="finger_izz"
                    value="${3.462e-06 if finger_type == 'probe' else (1.736e-05 if finger_type == 'bucket' else 1.999e-06)}"/>
    <xacro:property name="finger_ixz"
                    value="${-5.067e-06 if finger_type == 'probe' else (6.137e-07 if finger_type == 'bucket' else -4.098e-06)}"/>

    <!-- Reusable inertial block. -->
    <xacro:macro name="gripper_inertial" params="mass cx cy cz ixx iyy izz ixy:=0 ixz:=0 iyz:=0">
        <inertial>
            <origin xyz="${cx} ${cy} ${cz}" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <mass value="${mass}"/>
            <inertia ixx="${ixx}" ixy="${ixy}" ixz="${ixz}"
                     iyy="${iyy}" iyz="${iyz}" izz="${izz}"/>
        </inertial>
    </xacro:macro>

    <!-- Visual + collision from the same mesh, which is what every link here
         wants: these are small printed parts whose swept volume is the shape
         itself, and the CAD export is already coarse (200-1300 faces a link). -->
    <xacro:macro name="gripper_mesh_geometry" params="file">
        <visual>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/gripper_v2/${file}"/>
            </geometry>
            <material name="">
                <color rgba="1 1 1 1"/>
            </material>
        </visual>
        <collision>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
            <geometry>
                <mesh filename="${mesh_location}/gripper_v2/${file}"/>
            </geometry>
        </collision>
    </xacro:macro>

    <!-- Joint damping/friction, sized against the inertias above; see the long
         note in gripper_new.xacro for why they cannot be left at the 10/2 that
         file inherited. The links here are the same order of magnitude as that
         one's (27 g and 11 g bars, 19-43 g fingertips), so the same values
         hold: damping gives an explicit-integration limit of h < 2I/b well
         clear of the 1 ms world step, and friction stays under the links' own
         gravity torque so it cannot weld the linkage. Only Gazebo reads
         these. -->
    <xacro:property name="fourbar_joint_damping" value="0.01"/>
    <xacro:property name="fourbar_joint_friction" value="0.001"/>

    <!-- Mount unchanged from gripper_new.xacro. The base solid is the same
         part in the same frame, so the flange offset, the 180 deg yaw and the
         camera pose all carry over; see the header. -->
    <joint name="arm_gripper_base_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="link6"/>
        <child link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0522" rpy="0.0 0.0 3.141592653589793"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Gripper base: side plates, link6 flange, stand-offs, servo, pinion and
         all the pins and screw heads, merged into one solid. The servo carries
         its datasheet 90 g rather than the plastic density; the two are
         combined by parallel axis, so the tensor below is not a uniform-density
         one. -->
    <link name="arm_gripper_base_link">
        <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="0.2957" cx="-0.00033" cy="-0.00513" cz="0.04977"
                                ixx="4.228e-04" iyy="3.976e-04" izz="2.278e-04"
                                ixy="2.038e-06" ixz="5.816e-07" iyz="5.046e-05"/>
        <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="gripper_base.stl"/>
    </link>

    <!-- ================= driven bars (the sector gears) ================= -->
    <!-- The driver. Its sector gear meshes with the right-hand one, which is
         what makes the two jaws one degree of freedom; every other joint below
         mimics this one. Link frames throughout this file are parallel to
         arm_gripper_base_link at q = 0 and sit on their own pivot, so every
         joint origin is a pure translation and every axis is +/-Y. -->
    <link name="gripper_gear_left_link">
        <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="0.0269" cx="-0.00160" cy="0.00749" cz="0.00795"
                                ixx="1.222e-05" iyy="1.256e-05" izz="6.137e-06"
                                ixy="-4.439e-07" ixz="1.272e-06" iyz="2.212e-06"/>
        <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="gripper_gear_left.stl"/>
    </link>

    <joint name="gripper_gear_left_joint" type="revolute">
        <parent link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
        <child link="gripper_gear_left_link"/>
        <origin xyz="-0.024250 0.0 0.090470" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
        <limit effort="500" velocity="1.0"
               lower="${gripper_joint_lower_limit}" upper="${gripper_joint_upper_limit}"/>
        <dynamics damping="${fourbar_joint_damping}" friction="${fourbar_joint_friction}"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="gripper_gear_right_link">
        <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="0.0269" cx="0.00159" cy="0.00749" cz="0.00795"
                                ixx="1.222e-05" iyy="1.256e-05" izz="6.137e-06"
                                ixy="4.438e-07" ixz="-1.272e-06" iyz="2.212e-06"/>
        <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="gripper_gear_right.stl"/>
    </link>

    <!-- The gears mesh, so this bar turns the opposite way. That is carried by
         the axis, not by a negative multiplier, so the joint VALUES stay equal
         and a reader comparing joint_states sees one number. -->
    <joint name="gripper_gear_right_joint" type="revolute">
        <parent link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
        <child link="gripper_gear_right_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0.024250 0.0 0.090470" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 -1 0"/>
        <limit effort="500" velocity="1.0"
               lower="${gripper_joint_lower_limit}" upper="${gripper_joint_upper_limit}"/>
        <dynamics damping="${fourbar_joint_damping}" friction="${fourbar_joint_friction}"/>
        <mimic joint="gripper_gear_left_joint" multiplier="1.0" offset="0.0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ==================== coupler bars ==================== -->
    <!-- Second bar of each parallelogram. Same 50 mm length as the driven bar
         and its ground pivot is 35 mm from the gear axis, which is what holds
         the fingertip's orientation fixed. -->
    <link name="gripper_left_link">
        <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="0.0107" cx="-0.00492" cy="-0.00005" cz="0.02451"
                                ixx="5.063e-06" iyy="3.512e-06" izz="1.967e-06"
                                ixz="6.473e-07"/>
        <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="gripper_coupler_left.stl"/>
    </link>

    <joint name="gripper_link_left_joint" type="revolute">
        <parent link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
        <child link="gripper_left_link"/>
        <origin xyz="-0.007500 0.0 0.121200" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
        <limit effort="500" velocity="1.0"
               lower="${gripper_joint_lower_limit}" upper="${gripper_joint_upper_limit}"/>
        <dynamics damping="${fourbar_joint_damping}" friction="${fourbar_joint_friction}"/>
        <mimic joint="gripper_gear_left_joint" multiplier="1.0" offset="0.0"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="gripper_right_link">
        <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="0.0107" cx="0.00492" cy="-0.00005" cz="0.02451"
                                ixx="5.063e-06" iyy="3.512e-06" izz="1.967e-06"
                                ixz="-6.472e-07"/>
        <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="gripper_coupler_right.stl"/>
    </link>

    <joint name="gripper_link_right_joint" type="revolute">
        <parent link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
        <child link="gripper_right_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0.007500 0.0 0.121200" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 -1 0"/>
        <limit effort="500" velocity="1.0"
               lower="${gripper_joint_lower_limit}" upper="${gripper_joint_upper_limit}"/>
        <dynamics damping="${fourbar_joint_damping}" friction="${fourbar_joint_friction}"/>
        <mimic joint="gripper_gear_left_joint" multiplier="1.0" offset="0.0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- ==================== bar tips ==================== -->
    <!-- Massless frames on the far pivot of each bar. Both bars use the SAME
         offset because they are the same length - that is what a parallelogram
         means here, and it is why the gear tip and the link tip stay exactly
         35 mm apart at every q, matching the fingertip's own bore spacing. -->
    <xacro:macro name="fourbar_tip" params="name parent sign">
        <link name="${name}_link">
            <inertial>
                <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <mass value="0.001"/>
                <inertia ixx="1e-8" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="1e-8" iyz="0" izz="1e-8"/>
            </inertial>
        </link>
        <joint name="${name}_joint" type="fixed">
            <parent link="${parent}"/>
            <child link="${name}_link"/>
            <origin xyz="${sign * fourbar_tip_x} 0.0 ${fourbar_tip_z}" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
        </joint>
    </xacro:macro>

    <xacro:fourbar_tip name="gripper_gear_tip_left" parent="gripper_gear_left_link" sign="-1"/>
    <xacro:fourbar_tip name="gripper_gear_tip_right" parent="gripper_gear_right_link" sign="1"/>
    <xacro:fourbar_tip name="gripper_link_tip_left" parent="gripper_left_link" sign="-1"/>
    <xacro:fourbar_tip name="gripper_link_tip_right" parent="gripper_right_link" sign="1"/>

    <!-- ==================== fingertips ==================== -->
    <!-- The fingertip hangs off the gear-bar tip and mimics at -1.0, which
         cancels that bar's rotation exactly. That is not a fudge to keep the
         jaws looking parallel: in a parallelogram the coupler's orientation is
         invariant, so the fingertip's true motion IS a pure translation along
         a 50 mm arc, and -1.0 is the multiplier that reproduces it. The second
         bore rides on gripper_link_tip_*_link, which the closed loop keeps
         underneath it. -->
    <link name="gripper_bucket_left_link">
        <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="${finger_mass}" cx="${finger_com_x}" cy="0.0" cz="${finger_com_z}"
                                ixx="${finger_ixx}" iyy="${finger_iyy}" izz="${finger_izz}"
                                ixz="${finger_ixz}"/>
        <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="${finger_mesh_left}"/>
    </link>

    <joint name="gripper_bucket_left_joint" type="revolute">
        <parent link="gripper_gear_tip_left_link"/>
        <child link="gripper_bucket_left_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 1 0"/>
        <limit effort="500" velocity="1.0"
               lower="${gripper_bucket_joint_lower_limit}" upper="${gripper_bucket_joint_upper_limit}"/>
        <dynamics damping="${fourbar_joint_damping}" friction="${fourbar_joint_friction}"/>
        <mimic joint="gripper_gear_left_joint" multiplier="-1.0" offset="0.0"/>
    </joint>

    <link name="gripper_bucket_right_link">
        <xacro:gripper_inertial mass="${finger_mass}" cx="${-finger_com_x}" cy="0.0" cz="${finger_com_z}"
                                ixx="${finger_ixx}" iyy="${finger_iyy}" izz="${finger_izz}"
                                ixz="${-finger_ixz}"/>
        <xacro:gripper_mesh_geometry file="${finger_mesh_right}"/>
    </link>

    <!-- Mirror of the left fingertip joint. Its parent already turns the other
         way (see gripper_gear_right_joint's axis), so the -1.0 multiplier here
         cancels that rotation just as it does on the left. -->
    <joint name="gripper_bucket_right_joint" type="revolute">
        <parent link="gripper_gear_tip_right_link"/>
        <child link="gripper_bucket_right_link"/>
        <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 0.0" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
        <axis xyz="0 -1 0"/>
        <limit effort="500" velocity="1.0"
               lower="${gripper_bucket_joint_lower_limit}" upper="${gripper_bucket_joint_upper_limit}"/>
        <dynamics damping="${fourbar_joint_damping}" friction="${fourbar_joint_friction}"/>
        <mimic joint="gripper_gear_left_joint" multiplier="-1.0" offset="0.0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Visual markers on the closing pivot of each four-bar. Pure RViz aids:
         no collision, so they can never obstruct planning, and a negligible
         inertial only because MoveIt/KDL warns about inertia-less links on
         every start. A <cylinder> runs along its own local Z, and here local Z
         is NOT the pivot axis - these link frames are base-aligned, so the
         axis is Y and the pin has to be laid over onto it. -->
    <xacro:macro name="fourbar_marker" params="name parent">
        <link name="${name}">
            <inertial>
                <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <mass value="1e-6"/>
                <inertia ixx="1e-9" ixy="0" ixz="0" iyy="1e-9" iyz="0" izz="1e-9"/>
            </inertial>
            <visual>
                <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="0 0 0"/>
                <geometry>
                    <cylinder radius="0.001" length="0.030"/>
                </geometry>
                <material name="constraint_color">
                    <color rgba="1 0 0 0.5"/>
                </material>
            </visual>
        </link>
        <joint name="${name}_joint" type="fixed">
            <parent link="${parent}"/>
            <child link="${name}"/>
            <origin xyz="0 0 0" rpy="1.5707963267948966 0 0"/>
        </joint>
    </xacro:macro>

    <xacro:fourbar_marker name="fourbar_constraint_visual_left" parent="gripper_link_tip_left_link"/>
    <xacro:fourbar_marker name="fourbar_constraint_visual_right" parent="gripper_link_tip_right_link"/>

    <!-- Virtual TCP frame used as the IK tip. Held at gripper_new.xacro's
         0.15 m so the SRDF chain and every cached pose stay valid; it is a
         reference frame, not the contact point. The fingertips span
         z = 0.135-0.245 m from arm_gripper_base_link and the jaws actually
         meet at 243 / 215 / 224 mm for bucket / maintenance / probe - see the
         header. -->
    <xacro:property name="gripper_tcp_z" value="0.15"/>

    <link name="gripper_tcp"/>

    <joint name="gripper_tcp_joint" type="fixed">
        <parent link="arm_gripper_base_link"/>
        <child link="gripper_tcp"/>
        <origin xyz="0.0 0.0 ${gripper_tcp_z}" rpy="0.0 0.0 0.0"/>
    </joint>

    <!-- Gripper Camera (D435i) - defined in gripper_camera.xacro. Its mount
         pose is unchanged: it hangs off a bracket that is modelled in neither
         base mesh, and the base solid itself is the same part in the same
         frame, so the old extrinsics still apply. -->
    <xacro:include filename="gripper_camera.xacro" />

</robot>
